body:
This is somewhat related to https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/423 but a bit broader.

The current spec behavior for onerror is as follows:

1.  `.onerror` behaves identically on all windows/documents/nodes/`WorkerGlobalScope`s: all of them are `OnErrorEventHandler`.
2.  `.onerror` does NOT behave that same way on `Worker` and various other things. It uses `EventHandler` there.
3.  What gets passed to the handler depends on whether it's an `OnErrorEventHandler` _and_ whether the event is an `ErrorEvent`.
4.  During https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/webappapis.html#the-event-handler-processing-algorithm the return value handling depends solely on the event type and whether it's an `ErrorEvent` object.  It does not depend on the event handler type or event target.

Specifically, per spec, the following hold true:

* `onerror` on window/document/node/WorkerGlobalScope is invoked with 5 args if the event is `ErrorEvent`.
* `onerror` is invoked with 1 arg (the event), in all other cases (which means always on `AbstractWorker`).
* A true return value from an onerror handler does `preventDefault` on the event in all cases when the event is an `ErrorEvent` (including the worker case).

Observed browser deviations from the spec behavior seem to be:

Firefox: First bullet is false for document/element.  Third bullet is true not just for `ErrorEvent` but also for `Event`.
Safari:  First bullet is false for document/element.  Third bullet is false except for `ErrorEvent` dispatched on `Window` (and presumably `WorkerGlobalScope`?).
Chrome: First bullet is false for document/element.  Third bullet is true not just for `ErrorEvent` but also for `Event` _but_ false for objects other than `Window` (and presumably `WorkerGlobalScope`?).
Edge: Exactly like Chrome, as far as I can tell.

The testcase I used to determine this (using synthetic events) is this:

    <!DOCTYPE html>
    <pre id="log"></pre>
    <script>
      function log(str) {
        document.getElementById("log").appendChild(document.createTextNode(str+"\n"));
      }
    
      function stringifyArray(arr) {
        return String(arr.map((e) => String(e)));
      }
    
      var blob = new Blob([""]);
    
      var values = [true, false, "", "abc", {}, 0, 1, null, undefined];
      var targets = [window, document, document.getElementById("log"),
                     new Worker(URL.createObjectURL(blob))]
      var eventCtors = [Event, ErrorEvent]
      var curValue;
      var preventers;
      var nonpreventers;
      var calledAsOnErrorEventHandler;
      function classifier(event) {
        if (event.defaultPrevented) {
          preventers.push(curValue);
        } else {
          nonpreventers.push(curValue);
        }
      }
      for (ctor of eventCtors) {
        log(`Constructed using ${ctor.name}:`);
        for (target of targets) {
          log(`  Dispatching to ${target}:`);
          preventers = [];
          nonpreventers = [];
          for (var curValue of values) {
            // We have to make sure classifier is added _after_ our onerror event
            // handler.
            target.onerror = function(arg) {
              calledAsOnErrorEventHandler = (typeof arg == "string");
              return curValue;
            }
            target.addEventListener("error", classifier, false);
            var e = new ctor("error", { cancelable: true });
            target.dispatchEvent(e);
            target.onerror = null;
            target.removeEventListener("error", classifier, false);
          }
          log(`    calledAsOnErrorEventHandler: ${calledAsOnErrorEventHandler}`);
          log(`    event.defaultPrevented === true: ${stringifyArray(preventers)}`);
          log(`    event.defaultPrevented === false: ${stringifyArray(nonpreventers)}`);
        }
      }
    </script>

I did a bit of testing with non-synthetic events too.  For example, this testcase:

    onerror = function() {
      alert("onerror called");
    }
    var blob = new Blob(["nosuchfunction();"]);
    var w = new Worker(URL.createObjectURL(blob));
    w.onerror = function() { return true; }

shows an alert in Safari and Edge (consistent with them not treating "return true" as preventing default for onerror on `Worker` in my testcase above) but does not show an alert in Firefox (following the spec).  This testcase:

    onerror = function() {
      alert("onerror called");
    }
    var blob = new Blob(["nosuchfunction();"]);
    var w = new Worker(URL.createObjectURL(blob));
    w.onerror = function() { return false; }

shows the alert in Firefox but not Safari or Edge.  Neither one shows an alert in Chrome due to https://bugs.chromium.org/p/chromium/issues/detail?id=685303

Anyway, it's pretty clear that the spec does not match reality here at least for the document/element case...  Also that there's really crappy interop around error events on Worker.  :(

